data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_0306b39549bde8c2016ac678be1f5087d29acaf9edb914e513","logprobs":[],"output_index":0,"sequence_number":555,"text":"{\n  \"summary\": \"You discussed a payments-ledger migration and an idempotent payout API. Your recorded answers followed the suggestions you were shown and added useful technical detail, but were brief.\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    {\n      \"question\": \"Tell me about a migration of a critical data store you led.\",\n      \"type\": \"behavioral\",\n      \"how_it_went\": \"You named Acme, explained the MySQL-to-PostgreSQL migration, and added dual writes and a shadow read path beyond the suggestion. You did not explicitly say the migration was live or describe your leadership, validation, or results.\",\n      \"better_answer\": \"At Acme, I moved our payments ledger from MySQL to PostgreSQL using dual writes and a shadow read path. To complete this answer, add your specific responsibilities, how you verified correctness, and the actual outcome.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question\": \"How would you design an idempotent payout API?\",\n      \"type\": \"system_design\",\n      \"how_it_went\": \"You included the suggested idempotency key and added a state machine per payout. You did not explain how duplicate requests, concurrent retries, or failures would be handled.\",\n      \"better_answer\": \"I would require an idempotency key, atomically store it with the request parameters and payout record, and return the existing payout for matching retries while rejecting mismatched reuse. A durable payout state machine and downstream deduplication would prevent duplicate execution during concurrent requests and failure recovery.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"feedback\": \"Your answers were relevant and technically focused. Based on the recorded exchanges, your main opportunity is to move beyond naming techniques: show your ownership and outcomes for behavioral questions, and explain correctness and failure handling for design questions.\",\n  \"strengths\": [\n    \"You grounded the migration answer in a concrete payments-ledger example.\",\n    \"You added technical detail beyond both suggestions.\",\n    \"You answered directly without unrelated background.\"\n  ],\n  \"improvements\": [\n    \"For the migration, explain what you personally led and give the actual outcome.\",\n    \"Describe validation, cutover, and rollback decisions when discussing critical data migrations.\",\n    \"For idempotency, cover duplicate requests, concurrency, durable state, and downstream payout execution.\"\n  ],\n  \"follow_up_email\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Thank you for the conversation\",\n    \"body\": \"Hi,\\n\\nThank you for speaking with me. I appreciated the discussion about designing an idempotent payout API, particularly the role of idempotency keys and payout state management. I’m interested in continuing the conversation and learning about next steps.\\n\\nBest,\\nAlex Doe\"\n  }\n}"}

